prandtl-number-approximation.limits_and_boundary:
For fluids with very low Prandtl numbers (e.g., liquid metals, 
Pr < 1
), thermal diffusivity is much greater than momentum diffusivity (
α >> ν
). This means heat diffuses much faster than momentum, leading to a thermal boundary layer that is significantly thicker than the viscous boundary layer. Conversely, for fluids with very high Prandtl numbers (e.g., oils, polymers, 
Pr >> 1
), momentum diffusivity is much greater than thermal diffusivity (
ν >> α
). In this case, the viscous effects dominate, and the viscous boundary layer is much thicker than the thermal boundary layer. The approximation 
Pr ≈ 1
 represents the intermediate case where these two boundary layer thicknesses are of the same order of magnitude.
